---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
import Capture from '../components/Capture.astro'
import SurfaceLab from '../components/SurfaceLab.astro'
import ProductDetails from '../components/ProductDetails.astro'
import StartHere from '../components/StartHere.astro'
import { shot } from '../lib/content'
---

<Page>
  <section class="hero container" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Phosphor / the open-source workspace built on pi</p>
      <h1 id="hero-title">Your work.<br /><span>In context.</span></h1>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-summary">
      <p class="hero-lede">Many lanes. A clear next move.</p>
      <p>
        Run coding agents in parallel, review what changed, and keep the decisions worth
        remembering. From your Desktop to your own remote machines, the work stays inspectable.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="action action-primary" href="#install">Install Phosphor ↓</a>
        <a class="action action-text" href="/watch/">Watch the walkthroughs ↗</a>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-meta mono">
        <span>macOS · Linux</span><span>Free · MIT</span><span>Desktop + Host-backed PWA</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-proof">
      <Capture
        image={shot('home')}
        alt="Phosphor’s workbench showing fictional parallel lanes, their current activity and project usage."
        caption="One view of the work that needs you"
        eager
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="why" class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">01 / Work you can follow</p><h2>
            Parallel work.<br />A clear next move.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          A lane gives a task its own conversation and, by default in a Git repository, its own
          worktree. The local workbench keeps activity visible; the all-machines home brings
          together Needs you, Working and Recent.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="numbered-notes">
        <article id="transcript">
          <span>01 / INSPECT</span><h3>Read the work.</h3><p>
            Expand tool calls, inspect edits, and review Changes against the session baseline. A
            confident final answer is not your acceptance test.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article id="artifacts">
          <span>02 / KEEP</span><h3>Keep the deliverable.</h3><p>
            Plans, diagrams and HTML pages become versioned artifacts. Compare versions and return
            to them after compaction.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article id="sessions">
          <span>03 / CONTINUE</span><h3>Keep your place.</h3><p>
            Resume, fork or rewind a conversation. Steer an active turn or queue a follow-up without
            losing the thread.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <Capture
        class="workflow-chat"
        image={shot('chat')}
        alt="A fictional coding conversation with visible tool activity and the composer."
        caption="The transcript is the working record"
      />
      <div class="source-line">
        <a class="text-link" href="/guides/daily-work/">The daily workflow →</a><a
          class="text-link"
          href="/guides/#first-task">Start with one verifiable task →</a
        >
      </div>
      <div id="work"><SurfaceLab /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="brain" class="section alternate">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">02 / Brain · opt-in on Desktop</p><h2>
            Keep the reason.<br />Not just the result.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Queue a lane. Its decisions save when the read finishes. Start with your card and walk
          sentences between people and projects. Ask a question to light its trail on the map.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Capture
        image={shot('brain-graph')}
        alt="Brain’s graph and memory browser connecting fictional people, projects and decisions."
        caption="A graph you can question, with sources you can open"
      />
      <div class="numbered-notes">
        <article>
          <span>01 / OPT IN</span><h3>Choose what enters.</h3><p>
            Brain is off by default. Turn it on in Settings → Brain. Queue selected lanes; there is
            no silent sweep of your conversations.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>02 / FOLLOW</span><h3>Check the connections.</h3><p>
            Brain Agent checks new connections after reads settle. What changed shows each saved
            read, why Brain Agent made a change and what you can undo.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>03 / RECALL</span><h3>Bring context forward.</h3><p>
            Agents can ask a question, explore a name in context or trace how two things connect. A
            memory is earlier context, not an instruction.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <aside class="boundary-note">
        <strong>Local storage, not fully offline.</strong><p>
          Reading sends scrubbed lane text to the reader. Checks can send memories and excerpts to
          the Brain lane’s model. Brain stays on this Desktop, not on Hosts or in the web app.
        </p>
      </aside>
      <div class="source-line">
        <a class="action action-text" href="/guides/brain/">Set up Brain →</a><a
          class="text-link"
          href="/watch/#brain-walkthrough">Follow the walkthrough →</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="review" class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">03 / Review and return</p><h2>
            The next action.<br />With its evidence.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          The PR pill opens checks, reviews, threads and activity. Slack pills return to threads
          already in your conversation. Notifications bring you back to the lane that needs
          attention.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Capture
        image={shot('lane-pr')}
        alt="The lane PR inspector showing an illustrative pull request’s checks and review state."
        caption="Checks, review and merge readiness are separate stages"
      />
      <div class="split feature-detail">
        <div>
          <h3 class="subheading">Read-only on GitHub.<br />Deliberate in the lane.</h3><p>
            Fix and Address draft a hand-off into the composer; nothing is sent automatically.
            Missing checks are not passing checks. A Slack pill is a shortcut to a mentioned thread,
            not an inbox or a background Slack reader.
          </p><a class="text-link" href="/guides/daily-work/#merge"
            >PRs, Slack and notifications →</a
          >
        </div>
        <Capture
          image={shot('slack-pill')}
          alt="A Slack thread-link popup with fictional thread references from a conversation."
          caption="Return to the source conversation"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="machines" class="section alternate">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">04 / Machines and PWA</p><h2>Your computers.<br />Within reach.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Run pi on your own Host. Reach its lanes from Desktop or a phone browser over your private
          Tailscale network. The Host owns execution; the control service handles identity, never
          your lane contents.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Capture
        image={shot('machines')}
        alt="Phosphor’s Machines page with fictional Host readiness and connection details."
        caption="Select the machine before starting the work"
      />
      <div class="numbered-notes">
        <article>
          <span>01 / CONNECT</span><h3>Enroll deliberately.</h3><p>
            Install the Host, compare its fingerprint and sign in to providers there. An online
            machine is not automatically ready or authorized to run a lane.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>02 / CONTROL</span><h3>One controller.</h3><p>
            Read from the browser, take control explicitly, and use the shared chat and review
            surfaces. No local fallback when a Host is offline.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>03 / MOVE</span><h3>A copy, not magic.</h3><p>
            Desktop can move an idle lane with a checked snapshot plan. The original remains;
            credentials and running processes do not travel with it.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="source-line">
        <a class="action action-text" href="/guides/machines/">Connect a machine →</a><a
          class="text-link"
          href="/guides/machines/#browser">Use the PWA →</a
        ><a class="text-link" href="/guides/machines/#moves">Move a lane →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="swarm" class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">05 / Delegation with boundaries</p><h2>
            One mission.<br />More than one agent.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Independent tasks belong in separate lanes. Bounded pieces inside a mission can go to
          subagents through pi-subagents. Give the parent scope, ownership and acceptance criteria;
          inspect the results before integration.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Capture
        image={shot('remote-hosts')}
        alt="A fixture-driven Subagents pane demonstrating scoped tasks and parent coordination."
        caption="Delegation is inspectable, not a promise of correctness"
      />
      <aside class="boundary-note">
        <strong>Swarm is authorization, not an autopilot.</strong><p>
          The per-lane switch authorizes proactive delegation when useful. It does not launch a
          fixed team, assign models or approve a merge. Off removes future standing authorization;
          it does not stop existing children.
        </p>
      </aside>
      <div class="source-line">
        <a class="action action-text" href="/guides/swarm/">Work with a swarm →</a><a
          class="text-link"
          href="/guides/swarm/#case-study">A historical development example →</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <ProductDetails />
  <StartHere />
</Page>
